 "ROOT", "level": 1, "status": "expanded", "prior": 0.0, "posterior": 0.4864864864864865, "danger": 0.3, "actionability": 0.0, "explanatory_coverage": 0.0, "expand_score": 0.5, "evidence_for": ["Subacute worsening after decongestants is more consistent with foreign body (B1) than bacterial superinfection (B2), providing moderate support for B1 and mode…"], "evidence_against": ["Well-controlled asthma and lack of chronic symptoms slightly argue against B1 (foreign body) but do not eliminate it as an explanation for acute unilateral sym…"], "unresolved_questions": [], "children": ["B1.1", "B1.2", "B1.3"], "closure_reason": "", "turn_cost_to_refine": 1.0, "diagnosis_commitment_gain": 0.5, "interrupt_relevance": 0.2, "level_role": "family", "classification_axis": "etiology", "representative_diseases": []}, "B2": {"id": "B2", "label": "Infection Complicating Sinusitis", "parent": "ROOT", "level": 1, "status": "expanded", "prior": 0.0, "posterior": 0.36486486486486486, "danger": 0.2, "actionability": 0.0, "explanatory_coverage": 0.0, "expand_score": 0.5, "evidence_for": ["Fever and purulent discharge are more typical of infection (B2) than primary vascular lesions (B4), providing moderate support for B2 and moderate reduction fo…"], "evidence_against": ["Subacute worsening after decongestants is more consistent with foreign body (B1) than bacterial superinfection (B2), providing moderate support for B1 and mode…"], "unresolved_questions": [], "children": ["B2.1", "B2.2", "B2.3"], "closure_reason": "", "turn_cost_to_refine": 1.0, "diagnosis_commitment_gain": 0.4, "interrupt_relevance": 0.1, "level_role": "family", "classification_axis": "etiology", "representative_diseases": []}, "B3": {"id": "B3", "label": "Nasal/Paranasal Neoplasm with Unilateral Discharge", "level": 1, "status": "parked", "posterior": 0.014, "danger": 0.8, "parent": "ROOT", "closure_reason": "", "evidence_against": []}, "B4": {"id": "B4", "label": "Vascular/Structural Nasal Lesion with Epistaxis", "parent": "ROOT", "level": 1, "status": "live", "prior": 0.21739130434782614, "posterior": 0.13513513513513511, "danger": 0.5, "actionability": 0.0, "explanatory_coverage": 0.0, "expand_score": 0.0, "evidence_for": ["Active bleeding without trauma weakens B1 (foreign body) and provides moderate support for B4 (vascular lesion), as foreign bodies typically cause trauma and b…"], "evidence_against": ["Fever and purulent discharge are more typical of infection (B2) than primary vascular lesions (B4), providing moderate support for B2 and moderate reduction fo…"], "unresolved_questions": [], "children": [], "closure_reason": "", "turn_cost_to_refine": 1.5, "diagnosis_commitment_gain": 0.5, "interrupt_relevance": 0.3, "level_role": "family", "classification_axis": "etiology", "representative_diseases": []}, "B1.1": {"id": "B1.1", "label": "Retained Nasal Foreign Body (Organic) with Secondary Infection", "parent": "B1", "level": 2, "status": "live", "prior": 0.2918918918918919, "posterior": 0.2918918918918919, "danger": 0.25, "actionability": 0.0, "explanatory_coverage": 0.0, "expand_score": 0.0, "evidence_for": [], "evidence_against": [], "unresolved_questions": [], "children": [], "closure_reason": "", "turn_cost_to_refine": 1.0, "diagnosis_commitment_gain": 0.3, "interrupt_relevance": 0.2, "level_role": "disease_class", "classification_axis": "etiology", "representative_diseases": []}, "B1.2": {"id": "B1.2", "label": "Retained Nasal Foreign Body (Inorganic) with Mechanical Trauma", "parent": "B1", "level": 2, "status": "live", "prior": 0.14594594594594595, "posterior": 0.14594594594594595, "danger": 0.4, "actionability": 0.0, "explanatory_coverage": 0.0, "expand_score": 0.0, "evidence_for": [], "evidence_against": [], "unresolved_questions": [], "children": [], "closure_reason": "", "turn_cost_to_refine": 1.0, "diagnosis_commitment_gain": 0.4, "interrupt_relevance": 0.3, "level_role": "disease_class", "classification_axis": "etiology", "representative_diseases": []}, "B1.3": {"id": "B1.3", "label": "Nasal Foreign Body with Septal Perforation", "parent": "B1", "level": 2, "status": "live", "prior": 0.04864864864864866, "posterior": 0.04864864864864866, "danger": 0.7, "actionability": 0.0, "explanatory_coverage": 0.0, "expand_score": 0.0, "evidence_for": [], "evidence_against": [], "unresolved_questions": [], "children": [], "closure_reason": "", "turn_cost_to_refine": 1.5, "diagnosis_commitment_gain": 0.6, "interrupt_relevance": 0.5, "level_role": "specific_disease", "classification_axis": "anatomy", "representative_diseases": []}, "B2.1": {"id": "B2.1", "label": "Acute Bacterial Superinfection of Sinusitis", "parent": "B2", "level": 2, "status": "live", "prior": 0.18243243243243243, "posterior": 0.18243243243243243, "danger": 0.2, "actionability": 0.0, "explanatory_coverage": 0.0, "expand_score": 0.0, "evidence_for": [], "evidence_against": [], "unresolved_questions": [], "children": [], "closure_reason": "", "turn_cost_to_refine": 1.0, "diagnosis_commitment_gain": 0.4, "interrupt_relevance": 0.1, "level_role": "disease_class", "classification_axis": "etiology", "representative_diseases": []}, "B2.2": {"id": "B2.2", "label": "Fungal Sinusitis with Superimposed Bacterial Infection", "parent": "B2", "level": 2, "status": "live", "prior": 0.12162162162162163, "posterior": 0.12162162162162163, "danger": 0.5, "actionability": 0.0, "explanatory_coverage": 0.0, "expand_score": 0.0, "evidence_for": [], "evidence_against": [], "unresolved_questions": [], "children": [], "closure_reason": "", "turn_cost_to_refine": 1.5, "diagnosis_commitment_gain": 0.3, "interrupt_relevance": 0.4, "level_role": "disease_class", "classification_axis": "etiology", "representative_diseases": []}, "B2.3": {"id": "B2.3", "label": "Viral Sinusitis with Secondary Bacterial Infection", "parent": "B2", "level": 2, "status": "live", "prior": 0.060810810810810814, "posterior": 0.060810810810810814, "danger": 0.1, "actionability": 0.0, "explanatory_coverage": 0.0, "expand_score": 0.0, "evidence_for": [], "evidence_against": [], "unresolved_questions": [], "children": [], "closure_reason": "", "turn_cost_to_refine": 1.0, "diagnosis_commitment_gain": 0.2, "interrupt_relevance": 0.05, "level_role": "disease_class", "classification_axis": "mechanism", "representative_diseases": []}}, "frontier": ["B1.1", "B1.2", "B1.3", "B2.1", "B2.2", "B2.3"], "other_mass": 0.0, "pending_results": [], "actions_taken": [{"t": 1, "type": "ANALYZE_VIGNETTE", "content": "Does the unilateral purulent discharge with blood and persistent symptoms after decongestant treatment argue for a foreign body causing mechanical obstruction and secondary infection?", "summary": "Unilateral purulent discharge with blood in a child after decongestants is a classic sign of retained foreign body with superimposed infection, providing moderate support for B1."}, {"t": 1, "type": "ANALYZE_VIGNETTE", "content": "Does the presence of fever (37.5°C) and tachycardia (124 bpm) in a child with acute sinusitis provide stronger evidence for bacterial superinfection than for foreign body retention?", "summary": "Fever and tachycardia in acute sinusitis are more typical of bacterial superinfection (B2) than foreign body, providing moderate support for B2."}, {"t": 1, "type": "ANALYZE_VIGNETTE", "content": "Does the presence of active bleeding in the nasal discharge and absence of trauma history support a vascular/structural lesion over a foreign body?", "summary": "Active bleeding without trauma weakens B1 (foreign body) and provides moderate support for B4 (vascular lesion), as foreign bodies typically cause trauma and bleeding."}, {"t": 1, "type": "ANALYZE_VIGNETTE", "content": "Does the well-controlled asthma and absence of chronic unilateral symptoms argue against a foreign body as the primary cause?", "summary": "Well-controlled asthma and lack of chronic symptoms slightly argue against B1 (foreign body) but do not eliminate it as an explanation for acute unilateral symptoms."}, {"t": 1, "type": "ANALYZE_VIGNETTE", "content": "Does the subacute progression over days after initial decongestant treatment argue against a simple bacterial superinfection in favor of a foreign body source?", "summary": "Subacute worsening after decongestants is more consistent with foreign body (B1) than bacterial superinfection (B2), providing moderate support for B1 and moderate reduction for B2."}, {"t": 1, "type": "ANALYZE_VIGNETTE", "content": "Does the combination of fever and purulent discharge argue against a primary vascular lesion in favor of infectious causes?", "summary": "Fever and purulent discharge are more typical of infection (B2) than primary vascular lesions (B4), providing moderate support for B2 and moderate reduction for B4."}], "differential_history": [{"top": [["B1", 0.486], ["B2", 0.365], ["B1.1", 0.292]]}], "deliberation": {}, "interrupt": {"active": false, "reason": "No universal instability markers or time-critical syndromes are present in the current clinical scenario. The patient's vitals (BP 90/48 mmHg, HR 124 bpm, SpO2 98%) suggest mild tachycardia and fever but not shock or respiratory compromise. No airway obstruction, herniation risk, or uncontrolled hemorrhage is indicated.", "required_actions": []}, "termination": {"ready_to_stop": false, "termination_type": "continue", "reason": "Leading terminal branch posterior (0.292) is below 0.75 threshold, and dangerous alternatives (B4: posterior 0.135, danger 0.5) remain unresolved with actionable discriminators still available."}, "turn_budget_used": 1, "estimated_remaining_value": 0.9, "max_turn_budget": 5, "latest_action_type": "ANALYZE_VIGNETTE", "diagnosis_readiness_score": 0.2918918918918919, "benchmark_output_ready": false, "static_question": "", "static_options": [], "seen_evidence_ids": [], "seen_evidence_phenotypes": "{'Bloodstream infectious agent', 'Inappropriate absence of fever'}", "mode_policy": {"benchmark_purity": true, "allow_external_knowledge": true}, "answer_option_mapping": {}, "tool_use_log": [], "root_revision_needed": false, "max_tree_depth": 3, "reasoning_ledger": {"anchor": {"hypothesis": "B1", "t": 0, "posterior_at_anchor": 0.486}, "leader": {"branch_id": "B1.1", "label": "Retained Nasal Foreign Body (Organic) with Secondary Infection", "posterior": 0.292, "leading_since_t": 0, "n_revisions": 0}, "leader_evidence": {"confirming": 0, "disconfirming": 0, "last_disconfirming_digest": ""}, "action_intents": [{"t": 1, "intent": "broaden"}, {"t": 1, "intent": "broaden"}, {"t": 1, "intent": "broaden"}, {"t": 1, "intent": "broaden"}, {"t": 1, "intent": "broaden"}, {"t": 1, "intent": "broaden"}], "considered_alternatives": ["B2.1", "B1.2", "B4"]}, "discriminator_hints": "[Knowledge Layer: coverage=50%, source=dxs]\n\nRetained Nasal Foreign Body (Organic) with Secondary Infection vs Nasal Foreign Body with Septal Perforation:\n  Favours first:  epistaxis, foul smell from nose, headache, nasal discharge\n  Favours second: coughing, difficulty breathing, ear pain, nasal bleeding, swelling around the nose\n\nRetained Nasal Foreign Body (Organic) with Secondary Infection vs Acute Bacterial Superinfection of Sinusitis:\n  Favours first:  epistaxis, facial pain, foul smell from nose, nasal discharge, nasal obstruction\n  Favours second: bad breath, cough, ear pressure, facial pain or pressure, fatigue\n\nRetained Nasal Foreign Body (Inorganic) with Mechanical Trauma vs Nasal Foreign Body with Septal Perforation:\n  Favours first:  epistaxis, foul smell from nose, headache, nasal discharge\n  Favours second: coughing, difficulty breathing, ear pain, nasal bleeding, swelling around the nose\n\nRetained Nasal Foreign Body (Inorganic) with Mechanical Trauma vs Acute Bacterial Superinfection of Sinusitis:\n  Favours first:  epistaxis, facial pain, foul smell from nose, nasal discharge, nasal obstruction\n  Favours second: bad breath, cough, ear pressure, facial pain or pressure, fatigue\n\nNasal Foreign Body with Septal Perforation vs Acute Bacterial Superinfection of Sinusitis:\n  Favours first:  coughing, difficulty breathing, ear pain, facial pain, nasal bleeding\n  Favours second: bad breath, cough, ear pressure, facial pain or pressure, fatigue\n\n[RAG context for 'A 7-year-old boy is brought to the emergency department by his parents for worsening symptoms of previously diagnosed acute sinusitis' (score=0.65):]\n  Source: Clinical practice guideline (update): adult sinusitis. > History and Physical\n  Children show slight differences in the clinical signs of acute bacterial rhinosinusitis. Besides the 10-day duration, key symptoms, and double worsening, children are more prone to have fevers. Initi\n[RAG context for 'A 7-year-old boy is brought to the emergency department by his parents for worsening symptoms of previously diagnosed acute sinusitis' (score=0.66):]\n  Source: Epistaxis audit revisited. > Continuing Education Activity\n  Epistaxis, or nosebleed, is one of the most frequent ear, nose, and throat emergencies encountered in both primary care and emergency settings. It can be classified into anterior, which is more common\n[RAG context for 'Initially' (score=0.64):]\n  Source: Epistaxis audit revisited. > Introduction\n  These 5 vessels' watershed areas are in the anterior nasal septum, comprising Kiesselbach plexus. This lies at the entrance to the nasal cavity and is subject to extremes of heat, cold, and high and l\n[RAG context for 'Initially' (score=0.65):]\n  Source: Review of Clinical Practice Guideline: Nosebleed (Epistaxis). > Indications\n  The primary indication for posterior nasal packing is suspected epistaxis from a posterior source. Physical examination findings such as the absence of an anterior bleeding site on rhinoscopy, bleedin\n[Indirect reasoning chains (clinical inference):]\n  worsening symptoms of previously diagnosed acute sinusitis after initial decongestant treatment → secondary bacterial infection in acute sinusitis → Acute Bacterial Superinfection of Sinusitis\n    (frequency: occasional)\n    Suggestion: Evaluate for clinical signs of bacterial superinfection including purulent nasal discharge, fever progression, and lack of response to previous therapy\n  nasal discharge with worsening pain in a child with well-controlled asthma → asthma-related nasal mucosal vulnerability → Viral Sinusitis with Secondary Bacterial Infection\n    (frequency: occasional)\n    Suggestion: Assess for viral-bacterial coinfection patterns in asthmatic patients, including asthma exacerbation features alongside purulent discharge\n\nWhen indirect_reasoning_chains are present, generate candidates that investigate the INTERMEDIATE phenotype to confirm or rule out the indirect association."}
